The patch failed with `invalid JSON patch`. This is common when structuring complex strategic patches.

Let's re-examine the patch structure. The requirements for `runAsNonRoot` and `seccompProfile` apply to the Pod spec level, while the other two apply to the container spec level.

Pod level changes:
```json
{
  "spec": {
    "securityContext": {
      "runAsNonRoot": true,
      "seccompProfile": {
        "type": "RuntimeDefault"
      }
    },
    "containers": [
      {
        "name": "app",
        "securityContext": {
          "allowPrivilegeEscalation": false,
          "capabilities": {
            "drop": [
              "ALL"
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```
The issue might be in how the array structure is being represented or escaped in the shell command. I will use a simpler, more robust way to specify the patch, ensuring correct JSON syntax and array handling for capabilities.

I will try to patch the deployment using a more carefully structured strategic merge patch, ensuring all fields are correctly nested.

New attempt at patch structure:
```bash
kubectl patch deployment app -n restricted-app --type=strategic --patch '{
  "spec": {
    "template": {
      "spec": {
        "runAsNonRoot": true,
        "seccompProfile": {"type": "RuntimeDefault"},
        "containers": [
          {
            "name": "app",
            "securityContext": {
              "allowPrivilegeEscalation": false,
              "capabilities": {"drop": ["ALL"]}
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}'
```
I must ensure this entire JSON block is passed correctly as a single quoted string in Bash.

